sledgehammer — implementation plan
===================================

status: planned (2026-07-09)

references I1..I5 (plans/repl_list). pass-through ("sledgehammer" =>
keys ["repl", "timeout_secs"], default 15 already in the dispatcher).
two things distinguish it: it NEEDS external provers on the PATH
(spec: the scala process provides the bash bridge), and it is the
longest-running wave-1 call — the flagship beneficiary of I1's async
bridge.

advertised metadata (the builtin table row)
-------------------------------------------

name          sledgehammer
description   "Run Sledgehammer on the REPL's current proof state:
              external ATPs search for a proof and successful
              attempts come back as 'Try this: ...' lines with a
              one-liner you can pass to repl_step. Requires the REPL
              to be mid-proof (after a lemma statement). DO NOT set
              timeout_secs above 15 — the 15s default is almost
              always sufficient; Sledgehammer very rarely finds
              proofs beyond that."
              (source: mcp_server.py sledgehammer :359 verbatim,
              plus the mid-proof requirement and the Try-this reply
              shape of ir.ML:777.)
inputSchema   {"type": "object",
               "properties": {
                 "repl": {"type": "string"},
                 "timeout_secs": {"type": "integer", "default": 15}},
               "required": ["repl"]}
              (first tool with an OPTIONAL property — the schema's
              "required" omits it; the handler omits the pair when
              the client omits the argument, so the engine default
              stays in one place, the dispatcher.)
annotations   {"readOnlyHint": true, "idempotentHint": false,
               "openWorldHint": false}
              (read-only: run_sledgehammer only searches; the repl
              state is untouched — part of the readOnlyHint
              refinement family. NOT idempotent: ATP results vary
              run to run. openWorld stays false: external provers
              run as local subprocesses, not network services.)
outputSchema  none.

