genuine finding along the way, not part of the plan's own assumptions:
Ir.sledgehammer's deferred ML_Compiler.eval originally picked its
compilation context via `Toplevel.theory_of st` (the REPL's OWN base
theory, e.g. plain "Main"). Main is an ANCESTOR of MCP_Repl (which
declares the Ir structure via ML_file), not a descendant, so Main's
theory-local ML environment does not see Ir -- the eval failed with
"Structure (Ir) has not been declared" on every real call (masked
during earlier plans since sledgehammer had not been exercised
end-to-end before now). Fixed by capturing MCP_Repl's own theory value
ONCE at load time (Ir.set_self_theory, called from MCP_Repl.thy right
after `ML_file "ir.ML"`) and using that captured value -- rather than
the REPL's base theory -- as the eval context; MCP_Repl imports Main,
so its own theory is a strict superset (has both Sledgehammer and Ir).
A first attempt used `Thy_Info.get_theory "MCP_Repl"` (a by-name
lookup) instead of a captured value; that works under batch `isabelle
build` but fails against a live headless PIDE session (MCP_Session.start)
with "Theory loader: undefined entry for theory ..." -- such sessions
load theories through the document model, not the classical Thy_Info
loader, the same class of gap already on record for load_theory
(plans/repl_init T6, plans/load_theory). Capturing the theory value
directly sidesteps the loader and works uniformly in both contexts.

T1 (scala unit) confirmed the optional-arg plumbing: json_args already
only emits pairs for keys present in the client's arguments object, so
omitting timeout_secs reaches backend.ir as exactly [(repl,T)] and no
special-case handler code was needed -- the generic mechanism already
gives "the handler omits the pair when the client omits the argument"
for free. T2 (ml unit) confirmed a fresh REPL at theory level (not
mid-proof) fails sledgehammer cleanly with a status error. T3 (bridge,
tolerant of prover flakiness) found a proof for a trivial nat-commutativity
goal and the suggested one-liner closed it via a subsequent step. T4
decision: two concurrent sledgehammer calls would race the shared
sledgehammer_state/sledgehammer_result Synchronized slots (set, read,
cleared per call) -- serialized via a new global blocking lock
(Ir.with_sledgehammer_lock, ir.ML) rather than rejecting the second
caller with a busy error, since the dispatcher already runs each
request in its own fork_run future and a short wait is simpler for
callers than a distinct error to retry on; the lock only serializes
among sledgehammer calls, other REPL operations are unaffected. T5
confirmed a fast repl_list call returns while a slow sledgehammer call
is in flight on another REPL (shared pattern with plans/repl_step T5).
T6 (e2e) ran the full model-facing loop -- step a lemma, sledgehammer,
step the suggestion -- in test_mcp.py (test_agentic.py does not exist
in this repo, so e2e coverage lives alongside the other builtins'
e2e tests there, tolerant of prover flakiness as T3).

